[Proposition 8 of arXiv:2605.29030, 'Preferential relocations enhance survival \\ for Markov chains with killing'] Unique ergodicity holds for the kernel $\g$ given in \eqref{E:boldpi}  
for an arbitrary  $a\in (0,\infty)^S$ whenever either of the following two conditions are fulfilled:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] The law $\tau$ has a finite first moment, i.e.
\begin{equation} 
\sum_{j\geq 1} j \tau(j) <\infty.
\end{equation}

\item[(ii)]  The sub-stochastic matrix $\sigma$ is positive, i.e. $\sigma(s,t)>0$ for all $s,t\in S$, and the tail of $\tau$ satisfies 
\begin{equation} 
\sum_{i\geq n} \tau(i) =o(n^{-1/2}),\qquad \text{ as }n\to \infty.
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
Last, the defective kernel $\kk$ is H\"older-continuous whenever the tail of the law $\tau$ decays exponentially, i.e.
$$ \sum_{j\geq n} \tau(j) = o(b^{-n}), \qquad \text{for some }b>1.$$

