\end{enumerate}

[Defn def-bp-basic of arXiv:2411.06301, 'Inverting the operation of conditioning a branching process on extinction'] We say that $X$ is \textbf{positive regular} if there exists $N\in\mathbbm N$ such that 
\begin{equation*}
\mathbbm P_k\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft[ X_j(N) \geq 1 \aftergroup\egroup\originalright] > 0 ,\quad\forall k,j \in \{1,\dots,d\} ,
\end{equation*}
which is equivalent to the condition that the matrix $M^N$ has all positive entries.
We say that the branching process is \textbf{non-singular} if there exists $k \in\{1,\dots,d\}$ such that
\begin{equation*}
\mathbbm P_k\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft[ \sum_{j=1}^d X_j(1) \geq 2 \aftergroup\egroup\originalright] >  0 .
\end{equation*}

