body:
[Definition def:FPE-sol of arXiv:2604.25851, '{Non-uniqueness of nonlinear Markov processes in the sense of McKean associated with parab'] A \emph{distributional solution} (short: \emph{solution}) to (equation eq:FPE of the paper) with initial condition $\zeta \in \mathcal P$ is a weakly continuous curve of probability measures $\mu = (\mu_t)_{t> 0} \subseteq \mathcal P$ such that $\mu_t \in \mathcal P_*$ for all $t\in (0,\infty)$, $a(x,\mu_t)$ is non-negative definite for all $(t,x) \in (0,\infty)\times \mathbb{R}^d$, $(t,x)\mapsto a_{ij}(x,\mu_t)$ and $ (t,x)\mapsto b_i(x,\mu_t)$, $1\leq i,j \leq d$, belong to $L^1_{\textup{loc}}([0,\infty)\times \mathbb{R}^d; \mu_t dt)$, and for all $t>0$,
    \begin{equation}
        \int_{\mathbb{R}^d}\varphi \,d\mu_t - \int_{\mathbb{R}^d}\varphi\, d\zeta = \int_0^t \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} L_{a(\mu_s),b(\mu_s)}\varphi\, d\mu_s ds
    \end{equation}
    holds for all $\varphi \in C^2_c(\mathbb{R}^d)$. $\zeta$ is called \emph{initial condition} or \emph{initial datum} of $\mu$.

