{"artifact_digest":"sha256:ef264da578c65b1c2183ca396b78c3f4970a254790c444763de024ebe54a31fb","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/ef/ef264da578c65b1c2183ca396b78c3f4970a254790c444763de024ebe54a31fb.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:b298445c39ebf2e5473b1b05a03101f873f8b5670094ffdfdbc18de34a038085","bundle_entry_key":"2607-19511-theorem-8-cc","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_045","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s1_045.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2607-19511-theorem-8-cc","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: First suppose that $Q$ satisfies Conditional Consistency.  Since $c_G$ is realized by $Q$, we have $c_G(E) = Q([E, G]_\Pi)$.  Now fix $E \subseteq F \subseteq G$ with $E \in \cal F$ and $F, G \in \cal G$.  Then Equation~(eq-CC-product of the paper) holds, which becomes:
\begin{equation}
c_G(E) = c_G(F) \times c_F(E),
\end{equation}
so that $c$ satisfies the chain rule.  Conversely, suppose $c$ satisfies the chain rule.  Since:
\begin{equation}
