{"artifact_digest":"sha256:1514766fad566553e252f0c98769a3ed4009e40d59827cb0ca0e154f95818ca0","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/15/1514766fad566553e252f0c98769a3ed4009e40d59827cb0ca0e154f95818ca0.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:61b079210bba7e4eab89f97e649b8b91c55305109e1160483ab947d6acd11698","bundle_entry_key":"symmetric-triangle-mirror-start-efficient-coupling","bundle_id":"population.w344-open-problem-p0023-checked-partials-20261010","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"w344/bundle_003.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:probability.reflected_brownian:symmetric-triangle-mirror-start-efficient-coupling","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Burdzy's cached official open-problems page, Problem 4, asks about arbitrary distinct starting points in any acute triangle. The prior attempt gives only a spectral lower bound, with the correct generator convention. For a bounded reflection-symmetric triangle D, let R be reflection through a symmetry axis and start at x and Rx, with x off the axis. Run Neumann reflected Brownian motion X with generator Delta/2, set Y=RX until the first hitting time T of the axis, and set Y=X thereafter. Reflection invariance of the domain and the strong Markov property make both marginals reflected Brownian motion. At T, X=RX, so the construction is coalescent.

Before T, X lives in one half-triangle with Neumann condition on its exterior sides and absorbing condition on the axis. Its survival exponent is the first mixed-boundary eigenvalue lambda_mix of -Delta/2. Odd extension across the axis sends every admissible half-domain test function to a mean-zero H^1 function on D, preserving its Rayleigh quotient. Thus lambda_mix>=lambda_1, where lambda_1 is the first positive full-domain Neumann generator eigenvalue. The bounded-domain killed heat semigroup therefore gives, for each gamma<lambda_1, P_x(T>t)<=C_(x,gamma) exp(-gamma t). This is an affirmative special case for mirror-image starting points in reflection-symmetric acute triangles. It does not construct a coupling for arbitrary pairs or scalene triangles.

Checks: require a global domain symmetry, not just a reflected noise formula; identify absorbing/reflecting boundary pieces; require coalescence after meeting; use -Delta/2 rather than -Delta. A one-dimensional reflection control on [0,1], starting at 1/4 and 3/4, has generator exponent pi^2/2. Its exact mixed-boundary survival expansion has scaled limit (4/pi)cos(pi/4); three values are saved in special_case_measurements.jsonl. That control checks the convention and stopping mechanism, not the full triangular spectral theory.","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:4bc7956ea37dd6fa0ae41bf6874d628a55427cce12c88858150e8ffd982394f5","revision_digest":"sha256:08b3ac4141660dadad65bad58d200aa2b127bb34988304c484200d49180d7c21","source_id":"owner-w344-open-problems-20261010:symmetric-triangle-mirror-start-efficient-coupling","source_revision":"2026-10-10 worker result; proof; independent audit pending w346; novelty unverified","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

