{"artifact_digest":"sha256:8f1896a81ed5e3a84892055a5efc143c555f8bbd7959742d5cd7ce150713d486","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/8f/8f1896a81ed5e3a84892055a5efc143c555f8bbd7959742d5cd7ce150713d486.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:4095cc4fe111f4c285c35cfa6bc7b6d948b76ab989c14747977d9da252fe2fb5","bundle_entry_key":"2412-08502-lemma-5-s-tau","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_126","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s1_126.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2412-08502-lemma-5-s-tau","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: All the mentioned in the lemma inequalities are obvious in the case $k=1$.
Assume from now on that $k\ge2$ and consider for some fixed $a,b\ge 0$ and the expectation $\mathbb{E}[(a|S_\tau|+b);\tau=k]$. By the total probability law,
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}[(a|S_\tau|+b);\tau=k]
&\le \int_0^\infty \mathbb{P}(S_{k-1}\in dx,\tau>k-1)\mathbb{E}[(-aX+b);X\le-x]\\
&\le\sum_{j=0}^\infty \mathbb{P}(S_{k-1}\in [j,j+1),\tau>k-1] \mathbb{E}[(-aX+b);X\le-j]).
\end{align*}
Applying now Corollary~(cor:conc-tau of the paper), we get 
