body:
[Theorem thm-rapid-ordering-supercritical of arXiv:2607.22324, 'The conclave process'] {}
    Define $\lambda_k = 2^{1/k}$ for $k \geq 1$ and $\lambda_0=+\infty$. Suppose that $k \geq 2$ and $\alpha\in(\lambda_k,\lambda_{k-1}]$. Then for every $\epsilon > 0$ we have
    \begin{equation}{}
 		\lim\limits_{n\to \infty} \mathbb{P}_n^{\alpha}\left( \left| \frac{ \log(\alpha) \cdot \mathcal T}{ 2\log\log(n) } -1 \right|<\epsilon \right) =1 \,. 
 	\end{equation}
    In addition, suppose that $\alpha>2$ (corresponding to $k=1$ and $\alpha\in(\lambda_1,\lambda_0)$), and denote by $E_{\mathsf{unique}}$ the event that $\{ \mathbf X_1^{(i)}: 1 \leq i \leq n \}$ has a unique maximum. Then
    \begin{align}
        & \lim\limits_{n\to \infty} \mathbb{P}_n^{\alpha}\left( \left| \frac{ \log(\alpha) \cdot \mathcal T}{ 2\log\log(n) } -1 \right|<\epsilon \mid E_{\mathsf{unique}} \right) =1 \,;  \\
        & \lim\limits_{n\to \infty} \mathbb{P}_n^{\alpha}\left( \left| \frac{ \log(\alpha) \cdot \mathcal T}{ (1+\frac{\alpha}{2})\log\log(n) } -1 \right|<\epsilon \mid E_{\mathsf{unique}}^c \right) =1 \,. 
    \end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

