[Lem mild-strong of arXiv:2601.19145, 'Stochastic Persistence in Infinite Dimensions'] Fix $r \in [2,\infty), \theta \in \mathbb{R}$, $F: B \to \mathcal{B}_{\theta, r}$, $G: B \to \gamma(\mathcal{U},\mathcal{B}_{1/2+\theta, r})$, a stopping time $\tau$, and assume $x \in L^p(\Omega;C([0,T];B))$.
    If $x_t$ is a mild solution in $\mathcal{B}_{\theta,r}$ of (equation eq:gefx of the paper) up to time $\tau$ and there is some $\epsilon > 0$ such that
    \begin{equation}
        \|x_0\|_{\theta + \epsilon,r} + \mathbb{E}\Big[\int_0^{T \wedge \tau} \|F(x_t)\|_{\theta + \epsilon,r} + \|G(x_t)\|_{\theta + 1/2+\epsilon, r}^2dt\Big] < \infty \,,
    \end{equation}
    then $x_t$ is a strong solution in $\mathcal{B}_{\theta,r}$ up to time $\tau$. Conversely, if $x_t$ is a strong solution in $\mathcal{B}_{\theta,r}$ up to time $\tau$ and (equation eq:mild-vs-strong of the paper) holds, then $x_t$ is a mild solution in $\mathcal{B}_{\theta+1,r}$ up to time $\tau$.

