[Theorem 5 of arXiv:2605.23461, 'An almost sure invariance principle for the Takagi--van der Waerden class functions'] Suppose that \eqref{Assump2:A_n_to_infinite} and \eqref{Assump2:last_term} hold. We also assume that
  \begin{assumplist}[resume=assumptions]
    \item $\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} (a_{k+1} - a_k)^2 <+\infty$; or
    \item $\{a_k\}_{k\ge 1}$ is a monotone sequence.
  \end{assumplist}
  Then, for any $p\in (0, 1)$, without changing the distribution of the process $\{S_n \colon n\ge 0\}$, we can redefine the process $\{S_n\colon n\ge 0\}$ on a richer probability space on which a standard Brownian motion $\{B(t)\colon t\ge 0\}$ is defined such that
  \begin{equation}
    S_n - \sqrt{\frac{p}{1-p}} B(A_n) = o(A_n^{ \frac{1}{2} - \frac{\delta}{16} }) \quad \text{a.s.}
  \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma lem:M4 of arXiv:2608.26765, 'Beyond Client Averaging: A Client-Independent Second-Order Stationary-Bias Component in St'] There exists $C_H<\infty$ such that
\begin{equation}

  M_4:=\mathbb{E}[x^4]\le C_Hv_{\gamma,N}
  =C_H\left(\frac{\gamma^2}{N^2}+\gamma^3\right).
\end{equation}

