[Lemma lem:correlation_stopping of arXiv:2608.05089, 'Correlation decay in area-tilted line ensembles'] There is some $\lambda_0 > 1$ and $C, c > 0$ such that for all $\lambda \geqslant \lambda_0$
and all $k \in \mathbb{N}$, when $\mathbf{X}$ and $\mathbf{Y}$ are sampled independently we have
\begin{equation}
    \tau_\ell^{0,k} < 0 < \tau_r^{0,k}
    \qquad \text{and} \qquad
    \tau_\ell^{t,k} < t < \tau_r^{t,k}
\end{equation}
with probability at least $1 - C e^{-ct}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition def:admissible of arXiv:2608.26564, 'Real ideal points, Conway spheres, and left-orderable Dehn fillings'] An \emph{admissible real limiting datum} is a limiting gluing datum $\xi = (\chi_1, \chi_2)$ represented by irreducible homomorphisms $\rho_i: \pi_1(T_i) \to SL_2(\mathbb{R})$ such that, in the same-oriented strand variables,
\begin{equation}
	\rho_i(m_{i, 1}) = \rho_i(m_{i, 2}) = I \ \ \ \ \ \rho_i(m_{i, 3}) = \rho_i(m_{i, 4}) = P_i
\end{equation}
where $P_i$ is a noncentral parabolic of trace 2. Equivalently, in boundary variables, 
\begin{equation} \begin{array}{c|cccc} &\mu_{i,1}&\mu_{i,2}&\mu_{i,3}&\mu_{i,4}\\ \hline T_1&I&I&P_1^{-1}&P_1\\ T_2&I&I&P_2&P_2^{-1}. \end{array} \end{equation}

