{"artifact_digest":"sha256:84068aa3f64aed3d7deba3437f1d0b61637ecf5ea83b894ff4d5f194e5c4c41f","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/84/84068aa3f64aed3d7deba3437f1d0b61637ecf5ea83b894ff4d5f194e5c4c41f.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:a6b6f99dd48f19ddaaff61ac868d67b2f13b45fd9a2471eb6be8cec3801105f5","bundle_entry_key":"2608-24616-lemma-5-poissonized-ratio-moments","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_107","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_107.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2608-24616-lemma-5-poissonized-ratio-moments","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: The random measure $\sum_{j=1}^{N_t}\delta_{Y_j}$ is a Poisson random
measure on $[0,\infty)$ with intensity $t\mu_Y$. The Campbell--Mecke formula, or
equivalently the usual add-one-point argument for a compound Poisson sum,
gives
\begin{align*}
  m_t(p)
  &=t\,\E\left[
       \frac{Y^p}{(Y+B_t')^p}\1_{\{Y+B_t'>0\}}
     \right],
\end{align*}
where $B_t'$ is an independent copy of $B_t$, independent also of $Y$.
This is a standard Campbell--Mecke calculation; see, for example,
Kingman \cite[Chapter~3]{Kingman}.  For $z>0$,
\begin{equation*}
  z^{-p}=\frac1{\Gamma(p)}\int_0^\infty q^{p-1}e^{-qz}\,dq.
\end{equation*}
