[Lemma lem:coeff_s of arXiv:2509.15446, 'On the pair correlation function of the $\Sineb$ process'] Consider the functions $ \mathbf{s}_{j,\nu}(u), j\ge 0, u\ge \nu$ defined in (equation eq:s_nu_def1 of the paper). For every $\nu\in {\mathbb R}$, these are well-defined, and they satisfy the  ODEs given in (equation eq:coeff_s_ode1 of the paper) and   (equation eq:coeff_s_ode2 of the paper). Moreover, we have the following norm bounds for $\nu\le u$:
\begin{align}
        \|\mathbf{s}_{k,\nu}(u)\|\le e^{\frac{\beta}{4} k u} \,  \kappa^k \prod_{j=1}^k\frac{n}{j+\frac{4}{\beta}n},
\end{align}
with some $\kappa>0$ (which only depends on $n$). 
Moreover, these functions satisfy 
\begin{align}
    \lim\limits_{\nu\to -\infty} \mathbf{s}_{k,\nu}(u)=e^{k \frac{\beta}{4}u} \mathbf{s}_{k}.
\end{align}
With $\mathbf{q}_{\nu}(u)$ given by (equation eq:q_nu_series of the paper) we also have (equation eq:q_nu_lim of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition 5 of arXiv:2606.26619, 'Ergodicity of stochastic reaction-diffusion equations on unbounded domains driven by space'] Assume that there exists a constant $\beta\in\mathbb{R}$ such that $\sigma(\beta) = 0$. 
\begin{itemize}
  \item [(i)] Suppose that (H4) holds. If $\alpha > 0$, then for any $\lambda>0$ satisfying $\frac{\lambda^2}{2}<\alpha$, there exists a unique invariant measure in $L^1_{\lambda}$. Moreover, the solution is exponentially mixing with respect to the Wasserstein metric $W_1$.
  \item [(ii)] Suppose that (H2) and (H4) hold. If $\alpha > \frac{L_{\sigma}^4}{8}$, then for any $\lambda>0$ satisfying $\frac{\lambda^2}{8}<\alpha - \frac{L_{\sigma}^4}{8}$ and $\frac{\lambda^2}{4}<\alpha$, there exists a unique invariant measure in $L^2_{\lambda}$. Moreover, the solution is exponentially mixing with respect to the Wasserstein metric  $W_2$.
\end{itemize}

