[Lemma lem:Hanson:Wright of arXiv:2607.04047, 'Top Singular Value in Sum-Products of Random Matrices'] There exists some universal constant $c > 0$ such that for every $\epsilon > 0$ and $\theta \in \mathbb{R}^n$, 
\begin{align*}
    &\;
    {\mathbb P}\Big( 
        \,
        \Big|  
            \| X \theta \|^2
            -
            \Big(
                \mfrac{1}{m} \msum_{i \leq m} 
                \| X_{i,N-1:1} \theta \|^2
            \Big)
        \Big| 
        > \epsilon \, 
        \Big(
                \mfrac{1}{m} \msum_{i \leq m} 
                \| X_{i,N-1:1} \theta \|^2
            \Big)
    \Big)
    \;\leq\;
    2
    e^{
        - c n  \min\{ \epsilon^2, \epsilon\}
    }
    \;.
\end{align*}

