{"artifact_digest":"sha256:9b83dcbabe0d5638ea89ecd6a1b639bf67cacb7aca0c7dc0f9c4e2b140e5a376","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/9b/9b83dcbabe0d5638ea89ecd6a1b639bf67cacb7aca0c7dc0f9c4e2b140e5a376.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:90055d24c6404c554c5ae8a37af86031644be655b4077666398943fcc2029af6","bundle_entry_key":"2607-10486-lemma-49-lem-pn-martingale-solution","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_067","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_067.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2607-10486-lemma-49-lem-pn-martingale-solution","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Let $s,t \in [0,T]$ be fixed such that $s \leq t$. We aim to show that
     \begin{align}
       \mathbb{E}^{\mathbb{P}_n} \left(M_t^{n,z}-M_s^{n,z}\vert \mathscr{D}_s\right)=0.
     \end{align}
What we have proved is that 
\begin{align}
    \mathbb{E} \left(e^{i \langle \bX_n(t), z \rangle}-e^{i \langle \bX_n(s), z \rangle} - I_{s,t}^{n,z}(\bX_n) \vert \mathscr{F}_s\right)=0,
\end{align}
where $I_{s,t}^{n,z}: \bZ_T \to \mathbb{C}$. \\ 
Recall that the law of the random object $\bX_n : \Omega \to \bZ_T$ is equal to $\mathbb{P}_n$.
