body:
[Proposition 8 (Finite second-order tensor estimate) of arXiv:2606.16662, '2606.16662'] Let \(A,B,C,E\) be finite sets and set
\[
        \Lfin=2+|A|+|B|+|C|+|E|.
\]
Let \((g_a)_{a\in A}\) and \((h_b)_{b\in B}\) be independent standard real Gaussian families.  For a deterministic tensor \(H=(H_{a,b,c,e})\), define the random matrix
\begin{equation}
        Z_{e,c}=\sum_{a\in A}\sum_{b\in B}H_{a,b,c,e}g_ah_b .
\end{equation}
Define the four-cut norm
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathfrak R_4(H):=\max\bigl\{&
  \|H\|_{\ell^2_{a,b,c}\to\ell^2_e},
  \ \|H\|_{\ell^2_{a,b,e}\to\ell^2_c},\\
 &\|H\|_{\ell^2_{a,c}\to\ell^2_{b,e}},
  \ \|H\|_{\ell^2_{b,c}\to\ell^2_{a,e}}
\bigr\}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Then, for every \(p\ge2\),
\begin{equation}
        \bigl\|\|Z\|_{\ell^2_c\to\ell^2_e}\bigr\|_{L^p(\Omega)}
        \le C\bigl(p+\log\Lfin\bigr)\mathfrak R_4(H),
\end{equation}
where \(C\) is an absolute constant.  The same bound holds for complex Gaussian families after realification and, after the order-two decoupling in Lemma~\ref{lem:local-normal-form}, for arbitrary finite centered quadratic Gaussian chaoses.  It is also stable under the finite sign, real-Fourier and auxiliary-coordinate patterns produced there: each pattern is estimated separately, and a bounded enlargement of the Gaussian legs or a finite sum of pattern bounds changes only the implicit constant.

[Lemma l3.5 of arXiv:2603.14447, '\Large \bf Backward doubly stochastic differential equations with and without reflection u'] \sl
		Let $F:\mathbb{R}^n\longmapsto \mathbb{R}^n$ be a continuous function satisfying

		\begin{align*}
			\left(x^1-x^2,F(x^1)-F(x^2)\right)\leqslant 0,\qquad 	 x^1,x^2 \in \mathbb{R}^n.
		\end{align*}

		Then,  for any $\varepsilon>0$ and  $y\in\mathbb{R}^n$, there exists a unique $x=J^{\varepsilon}(y)$ such that
		\begin{align*}
			x-\varepsilon F(x)=y.
		\end{align*}

		Moreover,  for any $\varepsilon,\varepsilon^1,\varepsilon^2 >0$, and 	$x,x^1,x^2\in\mathbb{R}^n$,  we have

		\begin{align*}
			&	\big|J^{\varepsilon}(x^1)-J^{\varepsilon}(x^2)\big|\leqslant |x^1-x^2| 
			\quad\hbox{and}\quad 	\lim\limits_{\varepsilon\rightarrow 0}J^{\varepsilon}(x)=x,\\

			&	 \left(x^1-x^2,F^{\varepsilon}(x^1)-F^{\varepsilon}(x^2)\right)\leqslant 0\quad\hbox{and}\quad
			\big	|F^{\varepsilon}(x^1)-F^{\varepsilon}(x^2)\big|\leqslant \frac{2}{\varepsilon}|x^1-x^2|,  \\

			&	 |F^{\varepsilon}(x)|\leqslant |F(x)| \quad\hbox{and}\quad 
			\big(x^1-x^2,F^{\varepsilon^1}(x^1)-F^{\varepsilon^2}(x^2)\big)
			\leqslant (\varepsilon^1+\varepsilon^2)\big(|F(x^1)|+|F(x^2)|\big)^2,
		\end{align*}

		where $F^{\varepsilon}$  is the Yosida approximation of the function $F$ defined by
		\begin{align*}
			F^{\varepsilon}(x)=F(J^{\varepsilon}(x))=\frac{1}{\varepsilon} [J^{\varepsilon}(x)-x].
		\end{align*}

		Additionally, 
		for any set $\{x^{\varepsilon}\}_{{\varepsilon>0}}\subset\mathbb{R}^n$ and $x\in\mathbb{R}^n$, if
		$\lim\limits_{\varepsilon\rightarrow 0}x^{\varepsilon}=x,$ then
		\begin{align*}
			\lim\limits_{\varepsilon\rightarrow 0}F^{\varepsilon}(x^{\varepsilon})=F(x).
		\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem 13 of arXiv:2607.16145, 'The Memoryless Best-Choice Problem'] As $t\to 1$
\begin{equation}
f^*(t)\sim -\log (1-t).
\end{equation}
Consequently, $f^*$ is integrable, that is $F^*(1)<\infty$, and ${\mathbb P}[\tau^*=1]=e^{-F^*(1)}>0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Thm 4 (Universality of persistence on triangulable spaces) of arXiv:2607.27535, 'Universal topological statistics on \\ triangulated singular spaces'] Let $M \subset \RR^D$ be an admissibly triangulable space, let $f: M \rightarrow \RR_{\ge 0}$ be a good density, and let $\filt$ be either the Vietoris-Rips or \v Cech filtration. Then the following holds independently of $M$ and $f$:
    \begin{gather*}
        \lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} \frac 1n \overline{\Pi}_{k, \filt} ( nf ) = \overline{\Pi}_{k,\filt,d}^\univstar,
    \end{gather*}
    where $\overline{\Pi}_{k,\filt,d}^\univstar$ is the same universal limit as in Theorem \ref{thm:univ_euclidean}. In particular, this result holds for any compact $C^2$-embedded manifold $M$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

