body:
[Proposition prop:separation-cdia of arXiv:2608.29708, 'Intuitionistic and Constructive Modal Logics for Classical Modal Logicians'] (1) $\textsf{C}_\Diamond \in \mathcal{V}^e$. (2) $\textsf{C}_\Diamond \notin \mathcal{V}^i$.
  \begin{proof}
    (1) Take any f-model $\mathfrak{M} = (W, \leq, \sqsubset, V)$.
    Here, $\mathfrak{M}$ does not need to satisfy the $\Box$-p and $\Diamond$-p conditions.
    Then take any $\varphi, \psi \in \mathscr{L}_{\Box\Diamond}$ and $x, x'$ such that $x \leq x' \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \Diamond(\varphi \lor \psi)$.
    Then there is $y'$ such that $x' \sqsubset y' \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \varphi \lor \psi$.
    Here, either $y' \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \varphi$ or $y' \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \psi$,
    then either $x' \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \Diamond \varphi$ or $x' \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \Diamond \psi$, respectively,
    so $x' \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \Diamond \varphi \lor \Diamond \psi$.
    Therefore, $x \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \textsf{C}_\Diamond$.

    (2) Consider the model $\mathfrak{M} = (W, \leq, \sqsubset, V)$, where $W = \set{ x, x', y, y' }$,
    $\mathord{\leq} = \set{ (x, x), (x, x'), (x', x'), (y, y), (y', y') }$,
    $\mathord{\sqsubset} = \set{ (x, y), (x', y'), (y, y), (y', y') }$,
    and $V(p) = \set{ y }$, $V(q) = \set{ y' }$.
    The model $\mathfrak{M}$ is visualized in Figure (fig:kdia-countermodel of the paper).

    \begin{figure}[ht]
      \centering
      \begin{tikzpicture}
      \fill (0, 2) circle (2pt);
      \draw (0, 2) node[left]{$x$};

      \draw (2, 2) circle (2pt);
      \draw (2, 2) node[right]{$y \Vdash^i p$};

      \draw [thick, ->, squig, rel] (0,2) to (2, 2);
      \draw (1, 2) node[above]{$\sqsubset$};

      \fill (0, 4) circle (2pt);
      \draw (0, 4) node[left]{$x'$};

      \draw [thick, ->, rel] (0, 2) to (0,4);
      \draw (0,3) node[left]{$\leq$};

      \draw (2, 4) circle (2pt);
      \draw (2, 4) node[right]{$y' \Vdash^i q$};

      \draw [thick, ->, squig, rel] (0,4) to (2, 4);
      \draw (1, 4) node[above]{$\sqsubset$};

      \end{tikzpicture}
      \caption{The model $\mathfrak{M}$. $\circ$ denotes a $\sqsubset$-reflexive world.}

    \end{figure}

    It is easy to see that $w \Vdash^i_\mathfrak{M} \Diamond (p \lor q)$ for every $w \in W$,
    but $x \nVdash^i_\mathfrak{M} \Diamond p \lor \Diamond q$.
    Therefore, $x \nVdash^i_\mathfrak{M} \textsf{C}_\Diamond$.
  \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

