[Proposition thm|forComputer of arXiv:2504.08935, 'Higher-Order Derivatives of First-Passage Percolation with Respect to the Environment'] The following two implications hold for every $\omega\in\Omega$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] If $\sigma_j^a(\omega)\in E_j^C$, then $f(\sigma_j^b(\omega))=f(\sigma_j^a(\omega))$;
\item[(b)] If $\sigma_j^b(\omega)\in \hat E_j$, then $f(\sigma_j^b(\omega))=f(\sigma_j^a(\omega))+(b-a)$.
\end{enumerate}

[Corollary 9 (Search--validation phase law) of arXiv:2608.21496, '2608.21496'] If $m,N\to\infty$ with $m^2/N\to\tau\in(0,\infty)$, then
\begin{equation}
B_{m,N}\longrightarrow
B(\tau):=\vartheta_4\!\left(0,e^{-\pi^2/(4\tau)}\right).

\end{equation}
Define $\tau_\varepsilon=\inf\{\tau>0:B(\tau)\le\varepsilon\}$. After first taking the joint limit at fixed $\tau$ and then letting $\varepsilon\downarrow0$,
\begin{equation}
\tau_\varepsilon=\log\frac1\varepsilon
+\frac12\log\log\frac1\varepsilon
+\log\frac4{\sqrt\pi}+o(1).

\end{equation}
No uniform finite-$(m,N)$ statement is asserted for an arbitrary sequence $\varepsilon=\varepsilon_N$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

