body:
[Theorem Einaiiagapi of arXiv:2412.09290, 'Asymptotics of Harish-Chandra transform and infinitesimal freeness'] Let $A$ be a random Hermitian matrix of size $N$. Assume that for every finite $r$ one has
  \begin{equation}
    \lim_{N \rightarrow \infty} N \left( \frac{1}{N} \log \mathbb{E}[H \text{}
    C (x_1, \ldots, x_r, 0^{N - r} ; \lambda_1 (A), \ldots, \lambda_N (A))] -
    \sum_{i = 1}^r \Psi (x_i) \right) = \sum_{i = 1}^r \Phi (x_i), 
  \end{equation}
  where $\Psi, \Phi$ are smooth functions in a (complex) neighborhood of $0$ and the
  above convergence is uniform in a (complex) neighborhood of $0^r$. Then one has the following     limit:
  \begin{equation}
    \lim_{N \rightarrow \infty} N \left( \frac{1}{N^{k + 1}} \mathbb{E} \left[
    \sum_{i = 1}^N \lambda_i^k (A) \right] -\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_k \right)
    =\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\mu}}'_k, 
  \end{equation}
  where $(\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_k)_{k \in \mathbb{N}}$ are given by ((topg of the paper)) and
  \begin{equation}
    \ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\mu}}'_k := \sum_{m = 0}^{k - 1} \frac{k!}{m! (m + 1) !
    (k - m - 1) !}  \left. \frac{\mathrm{d}^m}{\mathrm{d} x^m} ((\Psi' (x))^{k - m
    - 1} \Phi' (x)) \right|_{x = 0} . 
  \end{equation}

