[Lemma 10 of arXiv:2604.05234, 'Quantitative propagation of chaos and universality for asymmetric Langevin spin glass dyna'] Let $\nu \in \mP(\mC_T)$. Then there exists a function $R_\nu\in L^2([0, T]^2)$ such that
\begin{equation}
  R_\nu(t, s) - H_\nu^t(t, s) = \beta^2 \int_s^t H^t_\nu(t, u) R_\nu(u, s) du = \beta^2 \int_s^t R_\nu(t, u) H^u_\nu(u, s) du,  

\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
    R_\nu(t, s) \leq Ae^{A(t - s)},

\end{equation}
for all $0 \leq s \leq t \leq T$. Moreover there exists a constant $C \in (0, \infty)$ depending only on $(\beta, A, T)$ such that for all $\xi, \nu \in \mP(\mC_T)$, we have
\begin{equation}

    \int_0^T\int_0^t |R_\xi(t, s) - R_\nu(t, s)|^2 ds\, dt \leq C \int_0^T\int_0^T |K_\xi(t, s) - K_\nu(t, s)|^2 ds\, dt.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Prop eq:controlressource of arXiv:2603.21634, 'Individual-based stochastic model with unbounded growth, birth and death rates: a tightnes'] The process defined in Section~(sec:inductive of the paper) verifies that
