[Definition cecdon1 of arXiv:2505.12187, 'Speeding up quantum Markov processes through lifting'] Let $\mathcal{N} \subset \mathcal{M}$ be finite-dimensional von Neumann algebras, and let $\sigma$ be a full-rank state on $\mathcal{M}$. A linear mapping $E_{\mathcal{N}}: \mathcal{M} \to \mathcal{N}$ is a \emph{conditional expectation} if it is completely positive and unital, and there holds 
    \begin{align} 
        E_{\mathcal{N}}(AXB) = A E_{\mathcal{N}}(X) B, \quad \forall X \in \mathcal{M},\ A, B \in \mathcal{N}\,.
    \end{align}
Further, such a mapping $E_{\mathcal{N}}$ is called a \emph{conditional expectation with respect to $\sigma$} if it is trace-preserving with respect to $\sigma$:
    \begin{align} 
        \tr(\sigma E_{\mathcal{N}}(X)) = \tr(\sigma X), \quad \forall X \in \mathcal{M}\,.
    \end{align}

[Corollary 5 of arXiv:2609.04143, 'The Geometry of Real Anisotropic Bohnenblust--Hille Constants'] Under the assumptions of Theorem~\ref{thm:main-E}, one has
\[
 \frac{\bigl(\log C_{\mathbf q^{(m)}}^{(m)}\bigr)}{m d_m}
 \longrightarrow\frac{\log2}{4}
\]
whenever either of the following conditions holds:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textup{(\alph*)},leftmargin=2.35em]
\item $y_1^\downarrow(\mathbf q^{(m)})\ge1/12$ for all sufficiently large $m$;
\item $y_{13}^\downarrow(\mathbf q^{(m)})/y_1^\downarrow(\mathbf q^{(m)})\to0$.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lem 35 of arXiv:2608.28148, 'Flows and homotopies of Banach Lie algebroids'] Let $c\colon [t_0,t_1]\to A$ be a smooth admissible curve and set $\gamma:=\pi\circ c$. The expression \eqref{eq:transport_operator} is independent of the local trivialisation. For fixed $c$ we obtain an $\R$-linear map 
 \begin{align}
 \mathcal{T}_c \colon \Gamma(\gamma^\ast A) \rightarrow \Gamma(c^\ast(TA)), b \mapsto \mathcal{T}_c(b).\end{align}
 Finally, if $f\colon [t_0,t_1]\to\R$ is smooth, then 
 \begin{align} \mathcal{T}_c(fb) = f\mathcal{T}_c(b)+\frac{df}{dt} b^V, 
 \end{align} 
 where $b^V$ denotes the vertical lift of $b$.

