[Thm supercor of arXiv:2608.30518, 'Grow-up rates of inhomogeneous semilinear heat equations in the critical dimension'] There exists $\tau>0$ such that
\begin{equation}

\Psi(r,t,\tau)\le \Psi_{1}(r) \quad \text{in $B_{1}^2$}
\end{equation}
for any $\widehat{T}<t$, where $\widehat{T}$ is that in Proposition (superprop of the paper). As a result, we have 
\begin{equation}

\Phi(\delta(t-\tau),t)\le C
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation*}
    \Phi\le C e^{-\mu_1 t} (\zeta_1\varphi_1+\zeta_1'(t)\nu r^{-4}\Gamma)
\end{equation*}
for any $t>\overline{T}$, where $C>0$ and $\overline{T}>0$ are independent of $t$.

