[Defn Radial ground solutions of arXiv:2505.14762, 'Multiple radial SLE($\kappa$) and quantum Calogero-Sutherland system'] For each radial link pattern $\alpha$, we choose Pochhammer contours $\mathcal{C}_1, \ldots, \mathcal{C}_n$ along which to integrate out the $\boldsymbol{\xi}$ variables. The integration is well-defined since the conformal dimension of $\Phi_\kappa(\boldsymbol{z}, \boldsymbol{\xi},u)$ is 1 at the $\boldsymbol{\xi}$ points, i.e. since $\lambda_b(-2 a)=1$. This leads to a new function of $\boldsymbol{z}$ defined by
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{J}^{(m, n)}_{\alpha}(\boldsymbol{z},u):=\oint_{\mathcal{C}_1} \ldots \oint_{\mathcal{C}_m}  \Phi_\kappa(\boldsymbol{z}, \boldsymbol{\xi},u) d \xi_m \ldots d \xi_1 .
\end{equation}

In angular coordinates, we obtain 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{J}^{(m, n)}_{\alpha}(\boldsymbol{\theta}):=\oint_{\mathcal{C}_1} \ldots \oint_{\mathcal{C}_m} \Phi_\kappa(\boldsymbol{\theta}, \boldsymbol{\zeta}) d \zeta_m \ldots d \zeta_1 .
\end{equation}

[Lem 10 of arXiv:2604.00190, '2604.00190'] For any strategy $\pi\in\un{\Pi}^\nu$ and $n\in\bN$, we have
\begin{align}
v_{\pi_n}(x, y)\leq \cV^{\nu, (n)} {v_0}(x, y)\leq V^{\nu}(x, y),\qquad (x, y)\in\bR\times E. 
\end{align}

Let $\mathbb{M} = [0, 1]^{\mathbb{N}}$ be the product space of countably many unit intervals equipped with the product Borel $\sigma$-algebra and product uniform measure $\mathbb{Q}$. The augmented Poisson process $\Psi$ is a Poisson point process on $\mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{A} \times [0, 1] \times \mathbb{M}$ with intensity measure $\gamma \lambda_d \otimes \Theta \otimes \lambda_{[0, 1]} \otimes \mathbb{Q}$. Each point $(x, a, t, u) \in \Psi$ represents a potential vertex $(x, a)$ along with continuous auxiliary uniform marks $t \in [0, 1]$ and $u = (u_1, u_2, \dots) \in \mathbb{M}$ used to determine the independent inclusion of incident simplices according to the connection functions $\varphi_j$.
\mathbb{M} is the countable product of [0, 1] with measure \mathbb{Q}
\Psi has intensity \gamma \lambda_d \otimes \Theta \otimes \lambda_{[0,1]} \otimes \mathbb{Q}

