[Theorem t41 of arXiv:2411.19433, 'Singular mean-field backward stochastic Volterra integral equations in infinite dimensiona'] Let condition $(A_{3})$ satisfy. Then, for every \( \varphi(\cdot) \in L^2_{\mathcal{F}}(0, b; H) \), the equation (equation 63 of the paper) admits a unique solution \( Y(\cdot) \in L^2_{\mathcal{F}}(0, b; H) \). Moreover, the subsequent estimate holds:
\begin{align}
\| Y(\cdot) \|_{L^2_{\mathcal{F}}(0,T;H)} \leq C \| \varphi(\cdot) \|_{L^2_{\mathcal{F}}(0,T;H)}. 
\end{align}

Let \( \Phi^{\prime}\) and \( \Psi^{\prime} \) satisfy condition $(A_{3})$, and let \( \varphi^{\prime}(\cdot) \in L^2_{\mathcal{F}}(0, b; H) \) and \( Y^{\prime}(\cdot) \in L^2_{\mathcal{F}}(0, b; H) \) be the solution of the MF-SVIE (equation 63 of the paper) corresponding to \( (\varphi^{\prime}, \Phi^{\prime}, \Psi^{\prime}) \). Then, the subsequent estimate holds:
\begin{align*}
&\quad \bigg[\mathbb{E} \int_0^b \vert Y(t) - Y^{\prime}(t) \vert_H^2 \bigg]^{1/2}\, dt \leq C\bigg\{\bigg[ \mathbb{E} \int_0^b \vert \varphi(t) - \varphi^{\prime}(t) \vert_H^2 \, \\
&+  \int_0^t \vert \Phi(t, s, Y^{\prime}(s), \mathbb{E}[Y^{\prime}(s)]) - \Phi^{\prime}(t, s, Y^{\prime}(s), \mathbb{E}[Y^{\prime}(s)]) \vert_H^2 \, ds \\
&+   \int_0^t \vert \Psi(t, s, Y^{\prime}(s), \mathbb{E}[Y^{\prime}(s)]) - \Psi^{\prime}(t, s, Y^{\prime}(s), \mathbb{E}[Y^{\prime}(s)]) \vert_H^2 \, ds\bigg] dt\bigg\}^{1/2}.
\end{align*}

[Lemma lem: borne factorielle permutations paires et impaires sans point fixe of arXiv:2605.28770, 'Cutoff profiles for conjugacy invariant random walks on symmetric groups'] Let $n\geq 2$. Let $X_n^{\textup{even}} \sim \ensuremath{\textup{Unif}}_{{\ensuremath{\mathfrak{A}}} _n}$ and $X_n^{\textup{odd}} \sim \ensuremath{\textup{Unif}}_{{\ensuremath{\mathfrak{S}}} _n\backslash{\ensuremath{\mathfrak{A}}} _n}$.  Then
\begin{equation}
    \left| {\ensuremath{\mathbb P}} (f_1(X_n^{\textup{even}}) = 0)-1/e\right| \leq \frac{1}{(n-1)!} \quad \text{ and } \quad \left| {\ensuremath{\mathbb P}} (f_1(X_n^{\textup{odd}}) = 0)-1/e\right| \leq \frac{1}{(n-1)!}.
\end{equation}

