body:
[Lemma when-does-u-go-to-zero of arXiv:2608.23202, 'Locally finite Fixed points of Branching Brownian Motion'] Let $f:\mathbb{R}\to  [0,\infty)$ be a non-negative measurable function supported in $[-K, \infty)$ and $\phi(x) = 1-e^{-f(-x)}$. Then, 
\begin{equation}
    u_\phi(t, \lambda t -x) \leq e^t \mathbb{E}[f(x+ B_t - \lambda t)],
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
     u_\phi(t, \lambda t -x) \leq u_M(t, \lambda t -x -K).
\end{equation}

In particular, for $f$ either compactly supported or of the type (equation Form of f of the paper), 
\begin{equation}
u_\phi(t, \sqrt{2}t - x) \to 0    
\end{equation}
uniformly over $x\leq c\log t $ as $t\to \infty$ for some small enough constant $c>0$.

