body:
[Theorem teoScaleFnUV of arXiv:2604.08889, 'Matrix Representations for Scale Functions of Spectrally Negative Lévy Processes with Rati'] Let $X$ be a L\'evy process as in (equation eqnXTrajectories of the paper), with $\sigma>0$ and $d\in \mathbb{R}$. 
Consider $\bm{G}: = \bm{C}^- + \bm{D}^{-+}\bm{\Psi}$ which, under the block structure given below in (equation eq:params_ubv of the paper)-(equation eq:params_ubv1 of the paper), takes the explicit form $\bm{G} = \begin{pmatrix} -a & \bm{b} \\ \bm{t} & \bm{T} \end{pmatrix}$. Define $\bm{\nu}:=(\Phi_q \bm{I}-\bm{T})^{-1}\bm{t}$ and $\bm{V}:=\binom{1}{\bm{\nu}}$. Let $\bm{e}_1^-:=(1,0,\dots,0)^\intercal\in\mathbb{R}^{p+1}$ be the first canonical basis vector.
Then, for any $x\geq 0$ and $q\geq 0$ such that $\psi'(\Phi_q)>0$ we have
\begin{equation}
W^{(q)}(x) = \frac{1}{\psi'(\Phi_q)}\Big(e^{\Phi_q x} - (\bm{e}_1^-)^\intercal\,e^{\bm{G}x}\,\bm{V}\Big), \qquad x \ge 0.
\end{equation}
The matrix $\bm{\Psi}$ can be 
recovered from the pair $(a,\bm{b})$ via
\begin{equation}
\bm{\Psi} = \frac{1}{\omega_{\lambda+q}}\begin{pmatrix}\omega_{\lambda+q} - a & \quad \bm{b}\end{pmatrix},
\end{equation}
where $\omega_{\lambda+q} := \big(\sqrt{d^2 + 2\sigma^2 (\lambda+q)} + d\big)\sigma^{-2}$, $\eta_{\lambda+q} := \big(\sqrt{d^2 + 2\sigma^2 (\lambda+q)} - d\big)\sigma^{-2}$, and $(a,\bm b)$ are a solution to the system of equations
\begin{equation}
\sigma^2a^2-2da-2(\lambda+q)+\sigma^2\bm b\bm t=0,
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\sigma^2\bm{b}\left(\big(a-2d\sigma^{-2}\big)\bm I-\bm{T}\right) =2\lambda\bm{\alpha}.
\end{equation}
Finally, such a pair can be obtained as the limit of the recursive equations
\begin{equation}
\bm{b}_n = \Big( \frac{2\lambda}{\sigma^2}\,\bm{\alpha} + (\omega_{\lambda+q}-a_{n-1})\bm{b}_{n-1} \Big) \big(\eta_{\lambda+q}\bm{I} - \bm{T}\big)^{-1}, \qquad n \ge 1,
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
a_n = \omega_{\lambda+q} - \frac{\sigma^2}{2\sqrt{d^2+2\sigma^2(\lambda+q)}}\Big((\omega_{\lambda+q} - a_{n-1})^2 + \bm{b}_n\bm{t}\Big), \qquad n \ge 1,
\end{equation}
with $a_0=\omega_{\lambda+q}$, $\bm{b}_0=\bm{0}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Corollary 12 of arXiv:2609.03793, 'Typical martingale diverges on a co-$\sigma$-porous set'] The set
\begin{equation*}
R\coloneqq\bs{\f\in \M_\infty; \s{\nu\in2^\N;\osc f_n(\nu)>0} \text{ is comeager in $(2^\N,\rhoexp)$}}
\end{equation*}
is co-1-porous in $\M_\infty$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem 1 (half-rate linear programming bound) of arXiv:2609.03736, '2609.03736'] With
\begin{equation}
 \delta_\pi=\frac12-\frac1\pi,

\end{equation}
we have
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{R_D(\delta_\pi)=\frac12,}

\end{equation}
while $R_D(\delta)<\frac12$ for every $\delta>\delta_\pi$.
Equivalently, the attained half-rate inverse distance is
\begin{equation}
 \delta_D\!\left(\frac12\right)=\delta_\pi
 =\frac12-\frac1\pi.

\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

Let (a_n) be a bounded sequence of nonnegative reals. Then the Cesaro averages (1/N) sum_{n=1}^{N} a_n tend to 0 if and only if there is a subset J of the positive integers of DENSITY ZERO such that a_n tends to 0 as n tends to infinity along the complement of J. In words: convergence in density is convergence to 0 after deleting a negligible set of indices. The same statement holds with a_n replaced by |b_n| for a bounded complex sequence (b_n), and it is the standard bridge between mean ergodic statements and pointwise ones along a density-one set.
Boundedness of the sequence is used in the easy direction;
nonnegativity is a normalisation (apply to |b_n|).

