body:
[Thm th:h-growth of arXiv:2605.21366, 'The Martin boundary of the Directed Landscape'] On a full-probability event, any eternal solution $\mathfrak h$ satisfies
\begin{align}
    \lim_{|y|\to\infty} |y|^{-1}\bigl(\sup_{t'\le t\le t''}\mathfrak h(y,t)-ay^2\bigr) = -\infty
    \qquad \text{for all $t'<t''$ and $a>0$ in $\mathbb{R}$}.
\end{align}
Furthermore, for any $x$ and $s<t$ in $\mathbb{R}$,
       \begin{align}
       \lim_{|y|\to\infty}|y|^{-1}\bigl(\mathcal L(x,s;y,t)+\mathfrak h(y,t)\bigr)=-\infty.
       \end{align}
Consequently, the set of maximizers of $y \mapsto \mathcal L(x,s;y,t) + \mathfrak h(y,t)$ is bounded.

