{"artifact_digest":"sha256:d73e9df54825163beaae30fec32cd7a304ad566981c7ad7fc629af65624ed834","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/d7/d73e9df54825163beaae30fec32cd7a304ad566981c7ad7fc629af65624ed834.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:6bac4538989cb99742dd21f0dedd4271debbb31996312e32d8ba8eb843b62d7c","bundle_entry_key":"2510-21186-theorem-3-momentsp2","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_094","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_094.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2510-21186-theorem-3-momentsp2","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: By the definition (equation matrixP of the paper) of $(p_{ij})$, we have
\[
\prod_{i,j=1}^n (p_{ij})^{a_{ij}} (\overline{p_{ij}})^{b_{ij}}
= (-1)^c
\prod_{k=1}^{n-1}
\bigl( x_k^{a_{k \bullet}+b_{\bullet k}} \overline{x_k}^{a_{\bullet k} +b_{k \bullet}} \bigr)
\cdot (1-x_n)^{s} (1-\overline{x_n})^{t},
\]
where
\begin{align*}
&c= \sum_{j=1}^{n-1} (a_{nj}+b_{nj}) + \sum_{i=1}^{n-1} (a_{in}+b_{in}), \qquad
s = a_{n \bullet} -\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} b_{\bullet j}, \qquad
t = b_{n \bullet} -\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} a_{\bullet j}.
\end{align*}
Since $|x_n|<1$ almost surely,
