{"artifact_digest":"sha256:eae2f543db3cc500d2e0bd52e8e07ed34e8bcc03b7977e013e1193897d6263e4","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/ea/eae2f543db3cc500d2e0bd52e8e07ed34e8bcc03b7977e013e1193897d6263e4.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:4b5cbca1f5ea501ea573177187c9d3095ff3cfe3aee583a5004cdebbda686d1f","bundle_entry_key":"2607-19873-lemma-39-lem-norm-comparison-001","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_076","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_076.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2607-19873-lemma-39-lem-norm-comparison-001","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Let $\sigma_{\mu} \in \mathfrak a^+$ be the Lyapunov vector of $\mu$ 
(see Theorem 10.9 of \cite{BQ16b}), so that, almost surely, 
\begin{align*}
 \frac{1}{n} \log a_{g_n \cdots g_1}  \xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}  \sigma_{\mu}. 
\end{align*}
Still by Theorem 10.9 of \cite{BQ16b}, if $\alpha$ is in $\Sigma^+$, 
then we have $\alpha(\sigma_{\mu}) > 0$. 
Therefore, by Theorem 13.17 of \cite{BQ16b}, we may find constants $\ee, c > 0$ such that for any $n \geq 1$ and $\alpha \in \Sigma^+$, 
\begin{align*}
\bb P \big( \alpha( \log a_{g_n \cdots g_1} ) \leq \ee n \big)
\leq c e^{- \ee n}. 
\end{align*}
