If $0<R<\rho_0(n)$, then we have $$\nabla^2v_B-|\nabla v_B|g>0\quad \mathrm{in}\ B_R.$$

[Lemma 9 of arXiv:2608.12291, 'When should one stop the most exciting game?\\ {\tiny Sequential Inference for win-marting'] Under Assumption \ref{asmp:baseline} it holds that
\begin{equation*}
    \mathcal{L}g(0+)=\mathcal{L}g(1-)=0.
\end{equation*}
Consequently, $\mathcal{U}(t)\subset(0,1)$ and $\mathcal{U}(t)$ is an interval for every $t\ge0$, where $\mathcal{U}(t):=\{x:(t,x)\in\mathcal{U}\}$.

[Proposition 7 (Radon-Nikodym Estimate) of arXiv:2607.09575, 'Probabilistic estimates for a system of\\noisy integrate-and-fire neurons'] Let $\mathcal{E}_t$ be the stochastic exponential defined as in \cref{prop: NOVIKOV CONDITION FOR THE STOCHASTIC EXPONENTIAL}. Then, for all $p > 1$ close enough to $1$,
	\begin{equation*}
		\E\left[\mathcal{E}_t^{1-p}\right] \le C,
	\end{equation*}
	where $C > 0$ is a constant that depends on $p,\,T,\,\mathfrak{K}$, the initial distribution $X_0$, and the bounds on $b$ and $\sigma$, but is independent of $i$ and $N$. Moreover, for any $q > 1$, we have
	\begin{equation*}
		\E\left[\nnorm{\hat{b}_t^{i}}^q \right] \le \tilde{C},
	\end{equation*}
	where $\tilde{C} > 0$ is a constant that depends on $q,\,T,\,\mathfrak{K}$, the initial distribution $X_0$, and the bounds on $b$ and $\sigma$, but is independent of $i$ and $N$.

