[Lemma scaling_plambda of arXiv:2509.23496, 'Crossing probabilities in geometric inhomogeneous random graphs'] Fix $p\in(0,1)$ and let $E$ be a finite-crossing event associated with some $(k,A)$. Then, for any decay exponent $1<\delta\le\infty$, we have
    \begin{eqnarray}
            p^k \mathbb P^{(\lambda)}(E)&\le \mathbb P^{(p\lambda)}(E) &\le \mathbb P^{(\lambda)}(E), \\
         (\tfrac{\mathtt c}{\mathtt C}p^{d\delta})^{k-1}\mathbb P^{(p^{-d}\lambda)}(E)&\le \mathbb P^{(\lambda)}(p^{-1}E)&\le \mathbb P^{(p^{-d}\lambda)}(E),
    \end{eqnarray}

    where the left-hand side in~(equation scaling_pE of the paper) uses $\mathtt c, \mathtt C$ from (equation eq:conditions_rho of the paper) for the soft profile and is set equal to zero for the hard profile. If $E$ is downward closed and $\gamma>0$, we also have
    \begin{equation}
    \mathbb P^{(p^{d(1/\gamma-1)}\lambda)}(E)\le \mathbb P^{(\lambda)}(p^{-1}E).
    \end{equation}

