\begin{align*}
\bE[B^n_s(t) B^n_s(t')] & = r_n^{-1} \bE\lt[(W(s+ r_n, t) - W(s, t)) (W(s+ r_n, t') - W(s, t'))\rt]\\
& = r^{-1}_n r_n (t \wedge t') = t\wedge t'.
\end{align*}
Since paths of $B^n_s$ are continuous on $[0, 1]$, which is inherited from that of $W$, we infer that $B^n_s$ is a standard Brownian motion.

