[Lemma 24 of arXiv:2506.10511, '2506.10511'] For all $d\geq 1$, $\beta>0$ and $L>0$, there exist constants $c_1, c_2>0$ {\rm(}depending on $d, \beta$ and $L${\rm)} such that for each $k\in\mathds{N}$ and $\varepsilon>0$,
\begin{equation*}
\mathds{P}[F_{k,L,\varepsilon}^c]\leq c_1\exp\left\{-c_2 2^{\varepsilon k}/k\right\}.
\end{equation*}
Furthermore, for each fixed $L>0$ and $\varepsilon>0$, almost surely, there exists a {\rm(}random{\rm)} $k_0\in\mathds{N}$ such that $\cap_{k>k_0}F_{k,L,\varepsilon}$ occurs.

