[Theorem thm:Markov-construction of arXiv:2604.25851, '{Non-uniqueness of nonlinear Markov processes in the sense of McKean associated with parab'] For each $z \in \mathbb{R}^d$, let $\mu^z = (\mu^z_t)_{t\geq 0}$ be a solution to the FPE (equation eq:FPE of the paper) with initial datum $\mu^z_0 = \delta_z$, satisfying (equation eq:L1-int-SP of the paper), such that
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*), font=\normalfont]
        \item $(t,z) \mapsto \mu^z_t$ is injective from $\mathbb{R}_+\times \mathbb{R}^d$ to  $\mathcal P$;

        \item for each $z \in \mathbb{R}^d$ and $s \in \mathbb{R}_+ \setminus \{0\}$, $\mu^z_{s+}$ is an extreme point in the set of solutions to ($\mu^z_{s+}$-linFPE) (in the sense of (def:lFPE-sol of the paper)) with initial condition $\mu^z_s$.
    \end{enumerate}
    Then, for each $z \in \mathbb{R}^d$, there exists a unique solution path law $P^z$ to the MV-SDE (equation eq:DDSDE of the paper) with one-dimensional time marginals $P^z_t = \mu^z_t$, $t \geq 0$. $\{P^z\}_{z\in \mathbb{R}^d}$ is a nonlinear Markov core for $\{\mu^z\}_{z\in \mathbb{R}^d}$, which is uniquely determined by $\{\mu^z\}_{z \in \mathbb{R}^d}$ and (equation eq:DDSDE of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition Full-Diamond Backtracking Intersection of arXiv:2608.12415, 'Sharp Phase Transition for Ellipsoid Fitting'] Let \(A\) and \(B\) be two \(M_\alpha\)-shapes. We say that the composition
\(A\cdot  B\) forms a \emph{full-diamond backtracking intersection} if
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \(U_A  \equiv V_B\), where \(U_A\) is the left boundary square
    vertex of \(A\) and \(V_B\) is the right boundary square vertex of \(B\);

    \item the two circle vertices of \(A\) receive the same set of labels in $[d]$ as those in $B$.
\end{enumerate}

[Proposition 44 (Balanced reduction) of arXiv:2606.16636, 'Free energy of non-convex multi-species spin glasses with centered Ising spins'] Assume that $\xi$ is balanced with respect to $\lambda$ in the sense of Definition~\ref{d.balanced_comparison_structure}. Let $f$ be the limit free energy, or equivalently the Hopf formula in Theorem~\ref{t.main}. Then, for every $t\geq0$ and every $q\in\mcl Q_2^\sS$,
\begin{equation}

    u(t,q^\lambda)\leq f(t,q)\leq \sum_{s\in\sS}\lambda_s u(t,q_s).
\end{equation}
In particular,
\begin{equation}

    f(t,0)=u(t,0),\qquad t\geq0.
\end{equation}

