body:
[Proposition 6 of arXiv:2608.12291, 'When should one stop the most exciting game?\\ {\tiny Sequential Inference for win-marting'] Under Assumption \ref{asmp:baseline}, it holds that
\begin{equation}
    \mathbb{E}[g(X^x_\tau)]-g(x)=\mathbb{E}\left[\int_0^\tau \mathcal{L}g(X^x_u)\,du\right],
\end{equation}
for any stopping time $\tau$ adapted to $\mathbb{F}$.
Specifically, the representation \eqref{eq:lagrange_formulation} holds.

[Lemma 7 of arXiv:2604.12317, 'Weak solution for distribution dependent SDEs driven by L\'{e}vy noise'] Let \( \left(\bar{\Omega}, (\bar{\mathcal{F}}_{t})_{t\geq 0}, \bar{\mathbb{P}}\right) \) and \( (\bar{X}_{t},\bar{L}_t) \) be a weak solution to DDSDE \eqref{EQ:MVSDE:101} with \( \mu_{t}:=\mathscr{L}_{\bar{X}_{t}}|\bar{\mathbb{P}} \). If the SDE
\begin{equation}
    \d X_{t}=b\left(t,X_{t}, \mu_{t}\right) \d t+ \d L_{t},
\end{equation}
has a unique strong solution \( X \) up to life time with \( \mathscr{L}_{X_{0}}=\mu_{0} \), then DDSDE \eqref{EQ:MVSDE:101} has a strong solution.

[Corollary cor:log:Hanson:Wright of arXiv:2607.04047, 'Top Singular Value in Sum-Products of Random Matrices'] There exists some universal constant $c > 0$ such that for every $\epsilon \in (0,1)$ and $\theta \in \mathbb{R}^n \setminus \{0\}$, 
\begin{align*}
    {\mathbb P}\Big( 
        \Big|  
            \log \| X \theta \|
            -
            \mfrac{1}{2} \log  
            \Big(
                \mfrac{1}{m} \msum_{i \leq m} 
                \| X_{i,N-1:1} \theta \|^2
            \Big)
        \Big| 
        \;\geq\; 
        \mfrac{\epsilon}{2(1-\epsilon)}
    \Big)
    \;\leq\;
    2 e^{-c n \epsilon^2 }
    \;.
\end{align*}

