body:
[Definition 2 of arXiv:2512.07202, 'Hitting Probabilities for Hypoelliptic Differential Equations Driven by Fractional Brownia'] The \textit{control distance function} associated with the vector fields $\{V_i\}_{i=1}^d$ is defined by\begin{equation}
d(x,y):=\inf\left\{\big\|\dot{h}\big\|_{L^2([0,1])}: \Pi_1(x,h)=y\right\}.
\end{equation}Here $\Pi_1(x,h)$ denotes the solution to the equation (\ref{density1}) at time $t=1$ with driving path $h$ and initial location $x$. The infimum ranges over all those $h:[0,1]\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^d$ which are absolutely continuous with $L^2$-derivatives and satisfy the condition in \eqref{eq:ODE}.

[Definition def.ConstantExtension_canonical of arXiv:2506.07158, 'The diffusivity of supercritical Bernoulli percolation is infinitely differentiable'] Given a realization $\mathbf{a}$ and ${F \subseteq E_d({\scaleobj{1}{\square}})}$, we define \emph{the canonical grain}
		\begin{align}
			[x]^F:= \left\{
			\begin{array}{ll}
				x, & x \in \mathscr{C}_{\operatorname{bc}}^F({\scaleobj{1}{\square}}),\\
				\arg \min_{y \in \mathscr{C}_{\operatorname{bc}}^\mathbf{a}({\scaleobj{1}{\square}})} \dist(y, \mathscr{O}^F(x)), & x \notin \mathscr{C}_{\operatorname{bc}}^F({\scaleobj{1}{\square}}) .
			\end{array}\right.
		\end{align} 
		Here we fix a lexicographic order for $\arg \min$. We also keep the convention $[x] \equiv [x]^{\emptyset}$.	

		A function $u : \{0,1\}^{E_d({\scaleobj{1}{\square}})} \times {\scaleobj{1}{\square}} \to \mathbb{R}$ follows the canonical grain extension rule if 	
		\begin{align}
			u(\mathbf{a}^F, x) = u(\mathbf{a}^F, [x]^F).
		\end{align}

[Proposition prop:phi of arXiv:2607.12564, 'Power-law and log-periodic degree tails for a family of probability generating function eq'] The series $\Phi(u):=\sum_{\ell\in\mathbb{Z}}\eta\bigl(\lambda^{\,u-\ell}\bigr)$ converges locally uniformly
on $\mathbb{R}$, and uniformly on $[0,1]$, to a continuous, strictly positive function of period $1$. Moreover, with
$N=\lfloor\log_\lambda x\rfloor$ and $\{\,\cdot\,\}$ denoting the fractional part,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
  x^{\rho+1}\sum_{n\ge0}\lambda^{-(\rho+1)n}w_j(x\lambda^{-n})
  &=\Phi(\log_\lambda x)-\sum_{\ell<-N}\eta(\lambda^{\{\log_\lambda x\}-\ell})\\
  &=\Phi(\log_\lambda x)+o(1)\qquad(x\to\infty),
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
the error being uniform in $\{\log_\lambda x\}$.

