{"artifact_digest":"sha256:47060963ea6b9016f52e16faee49d90dc6ffb3e10c42e39e093960211a285e30","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/47/47060963ea6b9016f52e16faee49d90dc6ffb3e10c42e39e093960211a285e30.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:ef996e5bbaf32f6ce193408f4e0730cfcb867309e0e69800f144284356ae7378","bundle_entry_key":"2509-06730-lemma-13-k-th-moment","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_059","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_059.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2509-06730-lem-13-k-th-moment","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Due to considering the $k$--th moment we now need the many--to--few lemma. This is tedious to state, so
    {we only present the consequences that we need, the precise formulation can be found in [harris_many--few_2017].}
    To state the many--to--few lemma, we need to describe the joint law of $k$ hyperbolic Brownian motions. We do this by describing the behaviour of $k$ marks $1,\ldots,k$.
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item We start with one particle carrying all marks. 
