[Proposition eqn:AEinclusion of arXiv:2504.08935, 'Higher-Order Derivatives of First-Passage Percolation with Respect to the Environment'] For every $j\in W$, the events $E_j$, $\hat E_j$, $A_j$, and $\hat A_j$ satisfy 
\begin{align}
&\sigma_j^a(A_j)=\sigma_j^a(A_j\cap\{\omega_j=b\}) 
=A_j\cap \left\{\omega_j=a\right\} 
=E_j\cap \left\{\omega_j=a\right\}; \\
&\sigma_j^b(\hat A_j) = \sigma_j^b(\hat A_j\cap\{\omega_j=a\})  
=\hat A_j\cap \left\{\omega_j=b\right\} 
=\hat E_j\cap \left\{\omega_j=b\right\}. 
\end{align}

[Theorem 17 of arXiv:2605.03851, 'The Relay Random Tree:\\A stochastic geometry approach of multihop relay in an urban visib'] \textbf{Length of the relaying zone.}

Let $\landsc '$ be a given landscape, and $(\alpha, \beta)\in \landsc'$ be a building. Denote by $\theta$ the blockage angle of $(\alpha, \beta)$ in $\landsc'$, and let $\tilde{\landsc}$ be a random landscape given by:
\begin{itemize}
    \item On the right of $(\alpha, \beta)$, it is equal to $\landsc'$: $\tilde{\landsc}\cap \{(x, y): x\geq \alpha\} = \landsc' \cap \{(x, y): x\geq \alpha\}$.
    \item On the left of $(\alpha, \beta)$, it is a Poisson building process of intensity $\lambda$ and height distribution $\mathcal{L}$: $\tilde{\landsc}\cap \{(x, y): x> \alpha\} \overset{(d)}{=}\landsc \cap \{(x, y): x> \alpha\}$.
\end{itemize}
Then, in $\tilde{\landsc}$, the ground length of $\zone(\alpha, \beta)$, that is $\ell_\theta(\zone(\alpha, \beta)) := \min(\alpha-\alpha_{sc}, \frac{\beta}{\tan\theta})$, has its CDF equal to:
\[t\mapsto 1- \mathds{1}_{t\leq \frac{\beta}{\tan\theta}}\exp\left(-\frac{\lambda}{\tan\theta}(\primi(\beta)-\primi(\beta-t\tan\theta))\right).\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Corollary cor:berrycanc_intro of arXiv:2607.09946, 'Statistics on Yau's conjecture: Variance asymptotics'] Let the assumptions of (thm:ape of the paper) prevail and assume in addition that: if the dimension is $n=2$, then $\eta_\ell\log\ell=\mathrm{O}(\ell)$. Then,
    \begin{equation} 
{\mathbb{V}\mathrm{ar}}\left( Y_M([\ell-\eta_\ell,\ell])\right)= \frac{1}{N^M_{[\ell-\eta_\ell,\ell]}}\cdot\begin{cases}
    \Theta(1), \ &\text{if $\alpha\in [0,1)$,}
    \\
    \mathrm{o}(1), \ &\text{if $\alpha=1$ and $(M,g)$ has no conjugate points.}
\end{cases}
    \end{equation}

[Lemma 21 (Fourier second-moment bounds for \(\Xone\) and \(\Xtwo\)) of arXiv:2608.29726, '\LARGE A Priori Estimates for Singular Fractional Stochastic Burgers Equations'] The kernels in \eqref{eq:Xone-chaos-kernel} and
\eqref{eq:Xtwo-chaos-kernels} have Fourier
second-moment densities satisfying, uniformly in \(\eps\),
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
 \mathcal C_{G_{\Xone,2}^\eps}(\zeta)
 &\lesssim
 \langle\zeta\rangle_\gamma^{
  -(\gamma+1)-2(2q-\delta)+}, \notag \\
 \mathcal C_{G_{\Xtwo,j}^\eps}(\zeta) & \lesssim
 \langle\zeta\rangle_\gamma^{
  -(\gamma+1)-2\min\{3q-\delta,q\}+}, \quad j\in\{1,3\}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}

