[Lemma 7 of arXiv:2609.03726, 'Inverse Scattering for Single Photons in Quantum Optics'] Let $n \geq 2$. Fix $\delta >1/2$ and an integer $l \geq 0$. There exists $k_\rho = k_{\rho, l, \delta}>0$ and a constant $C_{ \rho,l, \delta}>0$ such that for every $k > k_\rho$ and every $f \in H^l_\delta(\mathbb{R}^n)$, the limit
\begin{equation}
\psi^f := \lim_{\varepsilon \to 0^+}(P_k - i\varepsilon)^{-1}f
\end{equation}
exists in $H^l_{-\delta}(\mathbb{R}^n)$, and 
\begin{equation}
\|\psi^f\|_{H^l_{-\delta}} \leq C_{ \rho,l, \delta}\|f\|_{H^l_\delta}.
\end{equation}
Moreover, $\psi^f$ admits the Neumann series representation
\begin{equation}
\psi^f
=
\sum_{j=0}^\infty
(-R_k^0 V_k)^j R_k^0 f
\quad\text{in } H^l_{-\delta}(\mathbb{R}^n).
\end{equation}

