body:
[Proposition bad of arXiv:2412.02087, 'The spectral distribution of random graphs with given degree sequences'] [Bounds on ``bad" edges]

    Suppose there are $L$ distinct ``bad" edges with multiplicities $\beta_i\geq 1$. When conditioned on the first $r$ ``good" repeated edges, the following bound is achieved
    \begin{align*}
        &\mathbb{E}\left[\left|H_{r+p+1} - \frac{1}{D-1}\right|^{\beta_{r+p+1}}\cdots  \left|H_{r+p+L} - \frac{1}{D-1}\right|^{\beta_{r+p+L}}\middle|H_1, H_2, \cdots, H_{r} \right]\leq \frac{C_L}{(D-1)^{L}}.
    \end{align*}

[Lemma 7 of arXiv:2506.17061, 'Non-uniform bounds for non-normal approximation via Stein's method with applications to th'] For $z \ge 5$ and $\xi$ is a random variable satisfying $0\le |\xi|\le |\Delta|$. Then
		\begin{equation}
			\E(f_{z}(W))^2 \le \dfrac{B^p (p^{p/k}+\E|W|^{2p})}{(1+z)^{2p}},
		\end{equation}
		and
		\begin{equation}
			\E(f_{z}'(W+\xi))^2 \le \dfrac{B^p (p^{p/k}+\E|W|^{2p})}{(1+z)^{2p}}.
		\end{equation}

