[Theorem thm:main of arXiv:2603.14906, 'Four Levels of Thermodynamic Convergence of Singularly Perturbed Markov Semigroups'] In the sense of (def:thermo-conv of the paper), for every $t>0$ the family $(P_t^\varepsilon,\pi^\varepsilon)$ converges thermodynamically to $(\bar P_t,\bar\pi)$ as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Level~I} holds under (ass:standing of the paper);
\item \textbf{Level~II} holds under (ass:standing,ass:CDkappa of the paper);
\item \textbf{Level~III} holds under (ass:standing,ass:CDkappa,ass:coeff-weak of the paper);
\item \textbf{Level~IV} holds for this $t$ \emph{if and only if} (ass:locking of the paper) below holds for this $t$, under (ass:standing,ass:CDkappa,ass:coeff-weak of the paper).
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition 22 of arXiv:2603.27940, '\Large \bf Stability of supermartingale optimal transport problems'] $\tilde\pi^k$ is a (sub-probability) supermartingale coupling between $\hat\mu^k$ and its second marginal $\tilde\nu^k$.
In addition, \begin{equation}
AW_1\!\left(\tilde\pi^k,\ (1-\eps_k)\,\pi^{R,\alpha}\big|_{K\times\R}\right)\longrightarrow0.
\end{equation}
Moreover, for $k$ large enough, the second marginal $\tilde\nu^k$ satisfies the domination
\begin{equation}
(1-2\varepsilon)\,\tilde\nu^k\ \le\ (1-\varepsilon)\,\nu^{R,\alpha,k}.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

