[Definition 7 (positive-type coordinate and threshold graph) of arXiv:2609.03736, '2609.03736'] Choose a unitary $\mathsf G$-module $V$ with a unit
$\mathsf H$-fixed vector $\ell_o$.  For $x=g\mathsf H$, put
$\ell_x=g\ell_o$ and assume that the coordinate
\begin{equation}
 t(x,y)=\langle\ell_x,\ell_y\rangle

\end{equation}
is real.  Then $t$ is $\mathsf G$-invariant, of positive type, and normalized
by $t(x,x)=1$.  For $\tau\in\RR$, let $\mathfrak G_t(\tau)$ be the graph on
$X$ in which distinct $x,y$ are adjacent when $t(x,y)>\tau$.  Thus points
allowed to occur together in an independent set satisfy
\begin{equation}
 t(x,y)\leq\tau.

\end{equation}

[Definition of arXiv:2412.06246, 'The smallest singular value for rectangular random matrices with Lévy entries'] For fixed $\theta>0,m>0$ we define subsets $\mathbb{S}_p^{n-1}(\theta)$ and $\mathbb{S}_a^{n-1}(m)$ of $\mathbb{S}^{n-1}$ via
    \begin{enumerate}
\item The set $\mathbb{S}_p^{n-1}(\theta)$ of $\theta$-peaky vectors consist of unit vectors in $\mathbb{R}^n$ that have $\ell_\infty^n$ norm at least $\theta$. \item  A vector $y\in \mathbb{S}^{n-1}$ is called $m$-sparse if $|\operatorname{supp}y|\leq m$. We then call $y\in\mathbb{S}^{n-1}$ almost $m$-sparse if we can find $J\subset\{1,\cdots,n\}$ with cardinality bounded by $m$, and $\|y\chi_J\|\geq\frac{1}{2}$. We use the notation $\mathbb{S}_a^{n-1}(m)$ to denote the set of almost $m$-sparse vectors of $\mathbb{S}^{n-1}$.
\end{enumerate}

[Definition 29 (Wiener chaos, $N$-dimensional version) of arXiv:2605.14630, '2605.14630'] For any $n\geqs 1$, we denote by $\cH_n$ the subspace of $\cH$ 
spanned by the random variables 
\begin{equation}
 \setsuch{H_n(W(h))}{h\in \H, \norm{h}_\H = 1}\;.
\end{equation} 
For $n = 0$, $\cH_0$ is the set
of constants, which is isomorphic to $\R$. Then $\cH_n$ is called the 
\emph{homogeneous Wiener chaos of order $n$}. 
The \emph{inhomogeneous Wiener chaos of order $n$} is defined as 
\begin{equation}
 \cH_{\leqs n} = \bigoplus_{k=0}^n \cH_k\;.
\end{equation}

