[Thm 8 of arXiv:2606.24584, '2606.24584'] Let $D$ be an $n\times n$ irreducible doubly stochastic matrix. Then $D$ is cyclic  if and only if there exists a permutation matrix $P$ such that
\begin{equation}
    P^{\intercal}DP = K_{h} \otimes J_{n'}.
\end{equation}
where $h:=h(D)$ and $n'=n/h$. Moreover, in that case, $D$ is cyclic of order $h$.

[Definition d:strongly log-dominating of arXiv:2605.22803, 'Persistence of asymptotic variance under transport:\\ from hyperfluctuation to stealthy hy'] Let $\rho:[0,\infty)\to [0,\infty)$ be an increasing function that satisfies
\begin{equation}
    \int_0^\infty \frac{1}{r \rho(r) + 1} \, dr < \infty.
\end{equation}
Then $\rho$ is called a \textit{strongly log-dominating} function.

[Lem 7 of arXiv:2609.02327, 'On the counting function of square-full numbers'] {\cite{IV}(Vinogradov)}
			For $|t|\geqslant 10$, there exists an absolute constant $C$ such that in the region 
			\begin{equation*}
				\sigma \geqslant 1-\frac{C}{(\log |t|)^{\frac{2}{3}}(\log \log |t|)^{\frac{1}{3}}}
			\end{equation*}
			we have the bounds :
			\begin{equation}
				\zeta(\sigma+it)\ll \left (\log |t| \right)^{\frac{2}{3}+\epsilon}
			\end{equation}

			\begin{equation}
				\frac{1}{\zeta(\sigma+it)}\ll \left (\log |t| \right)^{\frac{2}{3}+\epsilon}
			\end{equation}
			for any $\epsilon>0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

