body:
[Definition of arXiv:2608.10415, 'Continuous random variables'] Let $A$ be a $C^*$-algebra, coming with a trace $tr:A\to\mathbb C$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item The elements $a\in A$ are called noncommutative random variables.

\item The moments of such a variable are the numbers $M_k(a)=tr(a^k)$.

\item The law of such a variable is the functional $\mu_a:P\to tr(P(a))$.
\end{enumerate}

[Lemma 18 of arXiv:2510.27589, 'Geodesic switches and exceptional times in dynamical Brownian last passage percolation'] Fix $[s,t]\subseteq \RR$. Almost surely, for every $r\in \cT_{\0}^{
,[s,t]}$, exactly one of the following hold.
   egin{enumerate}
  \item $\Gamma_{\0}^{
,r}=\Gamma_{\0}^{
,r^-}$.
  \item $\overline{\Gamma_{\0}^{
,r}\setminus \Gamma_{\0}^{
,r^-}}$ is an excursion about $\Gamma_{\0}^{
,r^-}$. 
  \item There exist points $p_1=(x_1,t_1), p_2=(x_2,t_2), p_3 = (x_3,t_3), p_4=(x_4,t_4) \in \Gamma_{\0}^{
