{"artifact_digest":"sha256:e92268b3dcac57091d9dfac03924114bdab5a74e36f100ce498434b439c12cab","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/e9/e92268b3dcac57091d9dfac03924114bdab5a74e36f100ce498434b439c12cab.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:245cd1301ed76dc8d17eb3de36f3087827a75bb4ca59e6050f6761f4de22ede8","bundle_entry_key":"2412-02087-lemma-13-decomposition-of-graphs","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_117","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s1_117.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2412-02087-lemma-13-decomposition-of-graphs","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Observe the fact that $\sum_{l=1}^n d_l^{w_i} \leq \sum_{l=1}^n d_l^{w_i'}\sum_{l=1}^n d_l^{w_i''}$ where $w_i'+w_i''=w_i$ for all $i$. Then, we may write
    \begin{align*}
    t_{G}
    &= \frac{D^{\frac{k}{2}}}{n^{\frac{k}{2}}}
    \prod_{i \in V} \sum_{l = 1}^n d_l^{w_i}
    \frac{1}{D^rD^\frac{3p}{2}D^L}  \\
