body:
[Defn of arXiv:2606.24385, 'Gradient Mean-Field Dynamics with Measure-Valued States: Well-Posedness, Chaos, and Long-T'] Let $\gamma$ be a Gaussian measure on $(E,\mathscr{B}(E))$. We define the mean $a_{\gamma}$, and the covariance $B_{\gamma}$ of $\gamma$ by
\begin{align}
&a_{\gamma}(f)\coloneqq \displaystyle\int_{E}f(x)\gamma({\rm d} x), \hskip 0,1cm f\in E^{\ast},\\
& B_{\gamma}(f,g)\coloneqq \displaystyle\int_{E}\left[ f(x)-a_{\gamma}(f)\right]\left[g(x)-a_{\gamma}(g)\right]\gamma({\rm d} x), \hskip 0,1cm f,g\in E^{\ast}.
\end{align}

[Definition e-family of arXiv:2412.08400, '\vspace{-1.0cm} Characterization of Exponential Families of \\ Lumpable Stochastic Matrice'] We say that the parametric family of irreducible stochastic matrices $$\mathcal{V}_e = \left\{ P_\theta \colon \theta = (\theta^1, \dots, \theta^d) \in \mathbb{R}^d \right\} \subset \mathcal{W}( \mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E}),$$ 
forms an exponential family (e-family) of stochastic matrices with natural parameter $\theta$ and dimension $d$, when
there exist a function $K \in \mathcal{F}(\mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E})$ and $d$ linearly independent functions\footnote{Although $G_1, \dots, G_d$ are technically cosets, we treat them as coset representatives in $\mathcal{F}(\mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E})$.} $G_1, \dots, G_d \in \mathcal{G}(\mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E})$, 
such that
    \begin{equation*}
        P_\theta = {\mathfrak{s}} \circ \exp \left(K + \sum_{i = 1}^{d} \theta^i G_i\right),
    \end{equation*}
    where $\mathcal{G}(\mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E})$ is the quotient space
\begin{equation*}
    \mathcal{G}(\mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E}) \triangleq \mathcal{F}(\mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E})/\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E}),
\end{equation*}
with $\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E})$ introduced in Definition~(definition:anti-shift-functions of the paper)
    and ${\mathfrak{s}}$-normalization follows from  Definition~(definition:s-normalization of the paper).

[Proposition prop:DoA of arXiv:2510.06891, 'Multivariate CLT for L\'evy processes: convergence rates without moment assumptions'] If a L\'evy process $\bm{X}$ is in the DoA of $\bm{Z}$ in~$\mathbb{R}^d$, then $\mathds{E}[|\bm{X}_1|^p]<\infty$ for any $p\in[0,2)$. 
A genuinely $d$-dimensional L\'evy process $\bm{X}$ is in DoNA of $\bm{Z}$ 
 if and only if $\mathds{E}[|\bm{X}_1|^2]<\infty$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2608.13732, '\textbf{On the First Hitting Time Problems \\ for Diffusion Processes: \\ Local Time-Space'] The cumulative distribution function $G(t,x)$ of the first-hitting time of the lower boundary $b(t)$ is given by
\begin{align}
   G(t,x)=\mathbb{P}_{T-t,x}\left(Y_T\le b(0)\right)-\frac{1}{2}\int_0^{t} f(u)\, q(T-u, b(u) ; T-t, x)\, du
\end{align}
for $t\in[0,T]$, where $f(t)=G_x(t, b(t)+) \,\sigma^2(t, b(t))$ and $q$ is the transition density function of the process $Y$.

