[Lemma lem:carpet-smoothing of arXiv:2607.24705, 'Derangetropy Operators'] For almost every $s$: $u_{s}$ is continuous, and for every
		$\varepsilon > 0$ there is $C_{s,\varepsilon}$ with
		\begin{equation}
			\Vert P_{N}u_{s}\Vert_{\infty}
			\le C_{s,\varepsilon}\,N^{-1/2+\varepsilon}
			\quad\text{for all dyadic } N,

		\end{equation}
		so that $u_{s}$ and $w_{s}$ belong to
		$C^{1/2-\varepsilon}(\mathbb{T}_{2})$ for every $\varepsilon > 0$.
		At every rational $s$ the conclusion fails: $u_{s}$ is a step
		function. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem th:main of arXiv:2411.04690, 'Asymptotic distribution of the derivative of the taut string accompanying Wiener process'] If a convex function $\varphi$ satisfies
\begin{equation} 
\int\limits_{\mathbb{R}}  \varphi(u) e^{-2\lambda|u|} du <\infty
\quad \textrm{for some } \lambda\in (0,r)
\end{equation}
or
\begin{equation} 
     \int\limits_{\mathbb{R}}  \varphi(u) \, p_{\infty,r}(u) du =\infty
\end{equation}
then
\begin{equation} 
  \frac{1}{T}  \int_0^T \varphi(\eta'_{T,r}(t))\, dt
  \xrightarrow{\mathbb{P}} \int\limits_{\mathbb{R}} \varphi(u) \,  p_{\infty,r}(u)\, du  
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation} 
   \liminf_{T\to\infty} \frac{1}{T}  \int_0^T \varphi(\eta'_{T,r}(t))\, dt
   \stackrel{\text{a.s.}}{\geq} \int\limits_{\mathbb{R}} \varphi(u) \,  p_{\infty,r}(u)\, du.    
\end{equation}

If a function $\varphi$ is continuous almost everywhere, locally bounded and satisfies power growth condition (equation eq:power_bound of the paper), then
\begin{equation} 
      \frac{1}{T}  \int_0^T \varphi(\eta'_{T,r}(t))\, dt
      \xrightarrow{\text{a.s.}} \int\limits_{\mathbb{R}} \varphi(u) \, p_{\infty,r}(u)\,du.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

