[Proposition 11 (Proposition 2.6 of \cite{DLV}) of arXiv:2607.08536, 'Soft edge limit of the Laguerre beta-ensemble at the lower edge'] Let $\psi_d$ be the solution of the equation $\Airyop \psi_d=0$ with Dirichlet initial condition $\psi_d(0)=0,\psi_d'(0)=1$. Then a.s.~$\psi_d\notin L^2(\R_+)$, and $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $\Airyop$. 

There exists a unique $L^2(\R_+)$ solution $\psi_\infty$ of the equation $\Airyop \psi_\infty=0$ with initial condition $\psi_\infty(0)=1$. The functions $\psi_d,\psi_\infty$ satisfy the Wronskian identity
 \begin{align}
\psi_\infty'(x) \psi_{d}(x)-\psi_\infty(x) \psi'_d(x)= -1.
\end{align}

The inverse operator $\Airyop^{-1}$ is an a.s.~Hilbert-Schmidt integral operator on $L^2(\R_+)$ with kernel
\begin{align}
\mathsf{K}_{\Ai}(x,y)=\psi_\infty(x) \psi_{d}(y) \ind(x\ge y)+\psi_{d}(x) \psi_\infty(y) \ind(x<y).
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma lem:InfiniteMixingBound of arXiv:2511.10630, 'Cutoff for generalised Bernoulli--Laplace urn models'] Let $C > 0$ and consider the induced chain $\mathbf{X}^{\textup{ind}}$ on $\mathrm{Centre}(C)$ with transition matrix $P^{\textup{ind}}$, and recall the Cheeger constant $\Phi_{\ast} = \Phi_{\ast}^{(n)}$. Let $\mathbf{X}^{\textup{ind}}$ have $\ell^{\infty}$-mixing time $t_{\textup{mix}}^{\infty,\textup{ind}}$. Then there exists some constant $C_0 > 0$ such that for all $\varepsilon \in (0, \frac{1}{4})$,
    \begin{equation}

        t_{\textup{mix}}^{\infty,\textup{ind}}(\varepsilon) \leqslant C_0 \ \log\Big(\frac{4}{\varepsilon}\Big) \frac{n}{\Phi_{\ast}^{(n)}}. 
    \end{equation}
    Moreover, for all $c > 0$, there exists some $R_0 > 0$, depending only on $d, m, C$, such that for all $r \geqslant R_0$, we get that
    \begin{equation}

        t_{\textup{mix}}^{\infty,\textup{ind}}(r) \leqslant c \frac{n}{\Phi_{\ast}^{(n)}} \ . 
    \end{equation}

