[Theorem thm: boundary of arXiv:2506.21466, 'The Markov property for $\varphi^4_3$ on the cylinder'] For every $f \in C^\infty(\mathbb T^2
)$, there exists $\mathcal{Z}^0(f) > 0$ such that
\begin{equation}
\lim_{T \rightarrow \infty}\mathcal{Z}_T^0(f) = \mathcal{Z}^0(f). 	
\end{equation}
Consequently, there exists a unique probability measure$\nu^0$ on $S'(\mathbb T^2)$ such that the measures $\nu^0_T$ converge weakly to $\nu^0$ as $T \rightarrow \infty$.  

Furthermore, there exists random variables $W^0_\infty$ and $Z^0_\infty$ defined on the probability space $(\Omega, \mathcal F, \mathbb P)$ such that
\begin{enumerate}
	\item[(i)] $\nu^0 = {\rm Law}_{\mathbb P}(W^0_\infty+Z^0_\infty)$,
	\item[(ii)] ${\rm Law}_{\mathbb P}(W^0_\infty) = \mu^0$,
	\item[(iii)] $Z^0_\infty \in H^{1/2-\kappa}_z$ almost  surely,
	\item[(iv)] $\mathbb E[\|Z^0_\infty\|_{H^{1/2-\kappa}_z}^2]\leq C$. 
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma lem:Key1 of arXiv:2606.19313, 'Stability of Khintchine-type inequalities via log-monotonicity'] Let $r_k=r_k(X)$ for a random variable $X$. Let $\alpha=(\alpha_1, \alpha_2, \ldots )$ be a weak partition of $q$ with at least two nonzero parts. The following bounds hold.

\begin{enumerate}
\item If $(r_k)$ is log-concave, then $\prod_ir_{\alpha_i}\geq r_1r_{q-1}\geq r_q$;\\

\item If $(r_k)$ is log-convex, then $\prod_ir_{\alpha_i}\leq r_1r_{q-1}\leq r_q$.
\end{enumerate}

