body:
[Theorem thm_limit_curves of arXiv:2608.09105, 'Longest convex chains with i.i.d.\ points'] Assume (equation p_cont of the paper) and (equation p_lower of the paper).
Then for every $\varepsilon>0$, there exists $\delta=\delta(\varepsilon,\mathfrak{p})>0$ such that
\eeq{ 
\limsup_{n\to\infty}\frac{1}{n^{1/3}}\log\mathbb{P}\big(\mathscr{L}_n^\mathsf{far}(\varepsilon)\geqslant\mathscr{L}_n-\delta n^{1/3}\big) < 0. 
}
In particular, the following statement holds with probability one.
For any choice of convex chain $\mathcal{C}_n\subseteq\mathcal{S}_n$ such that $\#\mathcal{C}_n \geqslant \mathscr{L}_n-\delta n^{1/3}$, we have
\eeq{ 
\limsup_{n\to\infty}\inf_{f\in\argmax J}\max_{(x,y)\in\mathcal{C}_n}|f(x)-y| \leqslant \varepsilon.
} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem general of arXiv:2602.18784, 'A note on the cooperative two-type SIR processes on Galton-Watson trees'] Consider the two-type SIR process on $GW (p)$ with parameters $\alpha_i$, $\beta_i$, $\mu_i$, $i=1,2$. At time $0$, the root is infected with both disease $A$ and disease $B$. All other vertices are susceptible to both $A$ and $B$. Let $m$ be the mean of $p$. If
    \begin{equation}
      \max\left\{\frac{\alpha_1}{\mu_1},\frac{\alpha_2}{\mu_2}\right\}\leq \frac{1}{m-1}
      \textnormal {,}
    \end{equation}
then the probability of survival is $0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem Upper bound of cumulants Wigner of arXiv:2411.11341, 'Ultra high order cumulants and quantitative CLT for polynomials in Random Matrices'] Let $\mathcal{X}\sim Wigner$.  Consider the polynomial $P(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{D})$ in ((def of P of the paper)) and the random variable $\mathbf{X}$ defined in ((object of the paper)).  Suppose that $\mathbf{C}_1$ is satisfied. 
Then there exists a constant $\theta_2 \equiv \theta_2 (K, M, \mathsf{C}, t) \in (0, 1)$ such that 
\begin{align}|K_r(\mathbf{X})|\leq \frac{r!^{3M}}{(\theta_2 \sqrt{N})^{r-2}}

\end{align}
for any $N$ and $r\geq 3$. If $\mathbf{C}_1'$ is additionally assumed, we get the sharper bound
\begin{align}|K_r(\mathbf{X})|\leq \frac{r!^{M}}{(\theta_3 \sqrt{N})^{r-2}}.

\end{align}
with some constant $\theta_3\equiv \theta_3(K,M,\mathsf{C}, t) \in (0,1)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 3 of arXiv:2608.30409, 'Gradient estimates and volume doubling for locally finite weighted graphs with $CD\psi(n,-'] Let $G=(V,E)$ be a locally finite weighted graph and let $\psi\in C^{1}(0,+\infty)$. Then for any function $0<f\in\mathbb{R}^{V}$, 
\begin{equation*}
    -\Delta^{\psi}f=\Gamma^{\psi}\left(f\right)-{\psi}^{\prime}\left(1\right)\frac{\Delta f}{f}.
\end{equation*}

