body:
[Definition def:HX-def of arXiv:2509.08928, 'The near critical random bond Ising model via embedding deformation'] Given $X$ a spinor on $\Upsilon^\times(G)$ satisfying (equation eq:3-terms of the paper), one can define the function $H_X$ up to a global additive constant on $\Lambda(G)\cup\diamondsuit(G)$ by setting
\begin{equation}

\begin{array}{rcll}
H_X(v^\bullet_p(z))-H_X(z)&:=&X(c_{p0}(z))X(c_{p1}(z))\cos\theta_z, & p=0,1,\\[2pt]
H_X(v^\circ_q(z))-H_X(z)&:=&-X(c_{0q}(z))X(c_{1q}(z))\sin\theta_z,& q=0,1,\\[2pt]
H_X(v^\bullet_p(z))-H_X(v^\circ_q(z))&:=&(X(c_{pq}(z)))^2,
\end{array}
\end{equation}
similarly to~(equation eq:cS-def of the paper) and~(equation eq:cS(z)-def of the paper).

[C 8 of arXiv:2506.09264, 'The bunkbed conjecture still holds for cactus graphs\\ and for graphs with certain biconne'] Let \(G\) be a graph, \(T \subseteq V(G)\), \(\vec{p} \in [0,1]^{E(G)}\) and \(v,w \in V(G)\). Moreover, let \(B^T \vcentcolon= B^T(G,\vec{p})\) denote a random graph as usual.\\
If there exists a set \(W \subseteq V(G)\) such that \(v \notin W\), \(w \in W\) and \(\partial_GW \subseteq T\), then \begin{center}\(\mathbb{P}\bigl( v_0 \overset{B^T}{\longleftrightarrow} w_0\bigr) \text{ }\text{ } = \text{ }\text{ } \mathbb{P}\bigl( v_0 \overset{B^T}{\longleftrightarrow} w_1\bigr)\).\end{center}
In particular, \(\mathscr{B}(G, T, \vec{p}, v, w)\) holds in this case.\vspace{6pt}

[Lemma G_ratio_asymptotics of arXiv:2507.22011, 'Random Lozenge Waterfall:\\Dimensional Collapse of Gibbs Measures'] The ratio of the prefactors $G$
	(equation eq:G_factor_conjugation of the paper)
	in the pre-limit barcode kernel
	has the following asymptotic behavior:
	\begin{equation}

		\begin{split}
			&\lim_{L\to\infty}
			q^{-3L(t-1-s)}\hspace{1pt}\frac{G(T_0+s,X_0)}{G(T_0+t-1,X_0)
			\mathsf{w}_{q,\kappa}(X_0-\tfrac {T_0+s}2+1)}
			\\&
			\hspace{120pt}=
			-
			\mathbf{i}^{t-s}
			q^{\frac12\binom {t-1}2-\frac{1}{2}\binom {s-1}2+1-s}
			\sqrt{
			\frac{-\kappa^2}{(1-\kappa ^2 q^{2-t})(1-\kappa ^2 q^{1-s} )}
			}
			.
		\end{split}
	\end{equation}

